These two concepts are usually discussed in transaction management in frameworks like Spring (Java).

1. Transaction Isolation

Isolation defines how one transaction is visible to other concurrent transactions.

It controls data consistency when multiple transactions run at the same time.

Common Problems Without Isolation

1️⃣ Dirty Read
Reading data that another transaction has not committed yet.

Example:

Transaction T1

UPDATE account SET balance = 900 WHERE id = 1;

Transaction T2 reads balance = 900 before T1 commits.

If T1 rolls back → T2 read invalid data.

2️⃣ Non-Repeatable Read
Reading the same row twice but getting different values.

Example:

T1:

SELECT balance FROM account WHERE id = 1;

T2 updates the balance and commits.

T1 reads again → value changed.

3️⃣ Phantom Read

Example:

T1

SELECT * FROM orders WHERE amount > 1000;

T2 inserts a new row that matches the condition.

T1 runs the same query again → new row appears.

Isolation Levels

1️⃣ Read Uncommitted (lowest)

Allows dirty reads.

2️⃣ Read Committed

Only committed data is visible.

3️⃣ Repeatable Read

Same rows return same data during transaction.

4️⃣ Serializable (highest)

Transactions run like sequential execution.

2. Transaction Propagation

Propagation defines how a transaction behaves when one transactional method calls another transactional method.

Example:

Method A (transaction)
   |
   ---> Method B (transaction)

Propagation decides whether B will use A's transaction or create a new one.

Common Propagation Types
1️⃣ REQUIRED (Most Common)

If a transaction exists → join it
If none exists → create new

Example:

ServiceA.methodA()  (Transaction started)
       |
       ---> ServiceB.methodB()

Method B joins the same transaction.

If B fails → whole transaction rolls back.

2️⃣ REQUIRES_NEW

Always creates a new transaction.

Example:

Method A transaction
       |
       ---> Method B (new transaction)

If B fails → only B rolls back, A can still commit.

Used in:

Logging

Audit

Notifications

3️⃣ SUPPORTS

If a transaction exists → use it
If not → run without transaction.

4️⃣ NOT_SUPPORTED

Always run without transaction.

Existing transaction is suspended.

5️⃣ MANDATORY

Must run inside an existing transaction.

If no transaction exists → exception.

6️⃣ NEVER

Must run without transaction.

If transaction exists → exception.

Simple Interview Difference
| Feature  | Isolation                                          | Propagation                                                  |
| -------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Purpose  | Control visibility between concurrent transactions | Control behavior when one transactional method calls another |
| Used for | Concurrency control                                | Transaction flow                                             |
| Example  | Dirty reads, phantom reads                         | REQUIRED, REQUIRES_NEW                                       |

Very Short Interview Answer

Isolation defines how transactions interact with each other when running concurrently.

Propagation defines how transactions behave when one transactional method calls another transactional method.